Lane Runner - a minimal 3D engine and an endless traffic game
=============================================================

Author : Andrew Rudov, SE-2408
Course : Computer Graphics, final project
Build  : OpenGL 3.3 core profile, GLFW + GLEW + GLM, C++17


1. What this is
---------------

Two separate things live in this directory:

  engine/  a small reusable 3D engine: window and input, shader loading,
           indexed meshes with a primitive builder, a camera, a forward
           renderer (one directional light, up to eight point lights, fog,
           procedural sky, procedurally generated textures) and a
           screen-space HUD.

  game/    a game written against that engine: a three-lane endless road,
           an arcade car controlled with the arrow keys, a day/night cycle
           and traffic that gets denser and trickier over time.

No deprecated immediate-mode OpenGL is used anywhere: everything goes
through VAOs, VBOs and GLSL 330 shaders.


2. How to build and run
-----------------------

    make
    ./laneRunner

`make run` does both. `make clean` removes build/ and the binary.
The binary loads shaders/ at start-up, so run it from the project root
(it also looks one and two directories up, so build/ works as well).

Required packages, Arch:

    sudo pacman -S base-devel glfw glew glm mesa

Debian/Ubuntu:

    sudo apt install build-essential libglfw3-dev libglew-dev libglm-dev

Command-line flags (used for the screenshots and for smoke testing):

    --frames N     quit after N rendered frames
    --shot FILE    write the last frame as a binary PPM
    --time T       start the day/night cycle at T in [0,1); 0.25 is noon


3. Controls
-----------

    Left / Right  (or A / D)   change lane
    Up            (or W)       accelerate
    Down          (or S)       brake
    R                          restart the run
    P                          pause
    F2                         save screenshot.ppm
    ESC                        quit


4. The HUD
----------

    top left      score (distance travelled plus 25 per overtaken car)
    below it      speed bar and the current speed in km/h
    top right     difficulty level, and a bar that fills as night falls
    under it      number of cars overtaken
    on a crash    final score, best score of the session, blinking strip
                  meaning "press R"

The HUD has no font: digits are drawn as seven-segment glyphs made of
rectangles, which keeps the HUD free of any font or image dependency.


5. Game rules
-------------

The player's car stays at z = 0 and the world scrolls towards it, which is
what makes the road endless. Traffic spawns 185 m ahead, always slower than
the player, and is recycled once it falls behind the camera. Road markings,
street lamps, trees and buildings are recycled the same way.

Difficulty rises one level every 18 seconds:

  * the player's base speed grows from 24 m/s up to a cap of 58 m/s;
  * the spawn interval shrinks from 1.15 s down to 0.30 s;
  * from level 3 on, traffic starts changing lanes on its own.

The spawner never blocks all three lanes at the same distance, so the run is
always survivable. Touching another car ends the run; R starts a new one.


6. The day/night cycle
----------------------

A full cycle takes 90 seconds. The sun direction is rotated around the
scene; its elevation drives everything else:

  * sky gradient, with an orange band near the horizon at dawn and dusk;
  * the directional light switches from the sun to a dim blue moon light
    once the sun is below the horizon, both fading out at the crossing so
    there is no visible pop;
  * ambient light, fog colour and fog density;
  * stars appear in the sky shader, and the moon disc is drawn opposite the
    sun;
  * headlights, tail lights and the street lamps turn into point lights and
    start glowing.

Eight point lights are the shader's budget: two headlights plus the six
street lamps nearest the car.


7. Engine notes
---------------

  engine/window.*    GLFW context, framebuffer resize, frame clock, key
                     edge detection, PPM screenshots
  engine/shader.*    compile/link with readable errors, uniform location
                     cache, asset lookup that also works from build/
  engine/mesh.*      Vertex {position, normal, colour, uv}, Mesh (VAO/VBO/EBO),
                     MeshBuilder that appends transformed boxes, planes,
                     cylinders and wedges into a single mesh and generates
                     the texture coordinates (see section 7a)
  engine/texgen.*    procedural RGBA textures: asphalt, grass, concrete, facade
  engine/texture.*   GL texture object: repeat wrap, mip chain, trilinear
                     filtering, 8x anisotropic filtering when available
  engine/camera.h    look-at view and perspective projection
  engine/renderer.*  per-frame environment upload, sky pass, per-object
                     draw with a Material {tint, emissive, specular,
                     shininess}
  engine/hud.*       screen-space rectangles, bars and seven-segment numbers

  shaders/scene.*    Blinn-Phong with one directional and eight point
                     lights, exponential-squared fog, optional texture
                     (albedo = vertex colour * tint * texture)
  shaders/sky.*      attribute-less full-screen triangle; the fragment
                     shader rebuilds the view ray from the inverse
                     view-projection matrix and draws the gradient, the sun,
                     the moon and a cell-hashed star field
  shaders/hud.*      pixel coordinates to NDC, flat colour


7a. Textures
------------

All textures are generated by the program at start-up (engine/texgen.cpp,
tileable value noise from an integer hash), so no image files are loaded and no
third-party texture assets are used.

  asphalt   road surface and edge lines: fine aggregate, pebbles, worn patches
  grass     ground plane
  concrete  curbs, with expansion joints
  facade    4 x 4 window grid; its alpha channel is a mask of lit windows

How it works:

  * MeshBuilder projects every face onto the plane of its dominant normal axis
    to get UVs. Road-like meshes keep v running along Z; the facade layout puts
    u horizontal and v vertical on every wall.
  * A Material carries texture, uvScale, uvOffset and textureGlow. The vertex
    shader passes the UVs on, the fragment shader multiplies the vertex colour
    by the texel (scene.frag, uniform sampler2D uTex).
  * Road, grass and curbs scroll: the UV offset follows the travelled distance
    (fract(distance / tile length)), so the ground visibly streams past the
    car and the speed is readable even without lane dashes.
  * Towers are one unit-cube mesh scaled per instance; uvScale is set to
    size / tile size, so windows keep the same real-world size on every tower,
    and each tower gets a different whole-window offset, so each one lights a
    different set of windows.
  * At night the lit-window mask drives an emissive term (textureGlow follows
    the same day/night curve as the street lamps), so towers glow in the dark.
  * Mip-mapping plus anisotropic filtering keep the asphalt sharp at grazing
    angles and remove shimmering in the distance.

8. Tested on
------------

Linux (CachyOS, kernel 7.x), g++ 16.2.1, Mesa with an Intel GPU, OpenGL 4.6
core profile. GLEW resolves its entry points through GLX, so on a Wayland
session the window is created on X11/XWayland automatically.


9. Screenshots
--------------

screenshots/day.png, sunset.png and night.png show the same scene at three
points of the day/night cycle. Press F2 in game to capture your own frame
into screenshot.ppm (convert it with, for example, `magick screenshot.ppm
shot.png`).


10. Third-party code and assets
-------------------------------

Libraries (linked, not bundled): GLFW (zlib/libpng licence), GLEW (Modified BSD
and MIT licences), GLM (MIT licence). There are no third-party models, textures
or shaders: every texture and shader in this repository is written for this
project.
